als/blocks.py:Block.diff\n##Searching for term \"pandas.DataFrame.interpolate\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/resample.py:Resampler.interpolate`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n767 | def interpolate(\n768 |         self,\n769 |         method: InterpolateOptions = \"linear\",\n770 |         *,\n771 |         axis: Axis = 0,\n772 |         limit: int | None = None,\n773 |         inplace: bool = False,\n774 |         limit_direction: Literal[\"forward\", \"backward\", \"both\"] = \"forward\",\n775 |         limit_area=None,\n776 |         downcast=lib.no_default,\n777 |         **kwargs,\n778 |     ):\n779 |         \"\"\"\n780 |         Interpolate values between target timestamps according to different methods.\n781 | \n782 |         The original index is first reindexed to target timestamps\n783 |         (see :meth:`core.resample.Resampler.asfreq`),\n784 |         then the interpolation of ``NaN`` values via :meth:`DataFrame.interpolate`\n785 |         happens.\n786 | \n787 |         Parameters\n788 |         ----------\n789 |         method : str, default 'linear'\n790 |             Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n791 | \n792 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally\n793 |               spaced. This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n794 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n795 |               given length of interval.\n796 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n797 |             * 'pad': Fill in NaNs using existing values.\n798 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic',\n799 |               'barycentric', 'polynomial': Passed to\n800 |               `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n801 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n802 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n803 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n804 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n805 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n806 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n807 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n808 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n809 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n810 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n811 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n812 | \n813 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n814 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n815 |             and defaults to 0.\n816 |         limit : int, optional\n817 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n818 |             0.\n819 |         inplace : bool, default False\n820 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n821 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, Optional\n822 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n823 | \n824 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n825 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n826 |             restriction.\n827 | \n828 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n829 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n830 |               (interpolate).\n831 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n832 | \n833 |         downcast : optional, 'infer' or None, defaults to None\n834 |             Downcast dtypes if possible.\n835 | \n836 |             .. deprecated:: 2.1.0\n837 | \n838 |         **kwargs : optional\n839 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n840 | \n841 |         Returns\n842 |         -------\n843 |         DataFrame or Series\n844 |             Interpolated values at the specified freq.\n845 | \n846 |         See Also\n847 |         --------\n848 |         core.resample.Resampler.asfreq: Return the values at the new freq,\n849 |             essentially a reindex.\n850 |         DataFrame.interpolate: Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n851 |         DataFrame.bfill : Backward fill NaN values in the resampled data.\n852 |         DataFrame.ffill : Forward fill NaN values.\n853 | \n854 |         Notes\n855 |         -----\n856 |         For high-frequent or non-equidistant time-series with timestamps\n857 |         the reindexing followed by interpolation may lead to information loss\n858 |         as shown in the last example.\n859 | \n860 |         Examples\n861 |         --------\n862 | \n863 |         >>> start = \"2023-03-01T07:00:00\"\n864 |         >>> timesteps = pd.date_range(start, periods=5, freq=\"s\")\n865 |         >>> series = pd.Series(data=[1, -1, 2, 1, 3], index=timesteps)\n866 |         >>> series\n867 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00    1\n868 |         2023-03-01 07:00:01   -1\n869 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02    2\n870 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03    1\n871 |         2023-03-01 07:00:04    3\n872 |         Freq: s, dtype: int64\n873 | \n874 |         Downsample the dataframe to 0.5Hz by providing the period time of 2s.\n875 | \n876 |         >>> series.resample(\"2s\").interpolate(\"linear\")\n877 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00    1\n878 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02    2\n879 |         2023-03-01 07:00:04    3\n880 |         Freq: 2s, dtype: int64\n881 | \n882 |         Upsample the dataframe to 2Hz by providing the period time of 500ms.\n883 | \n884 |         >>> series.resample(\"500ms\").interpolate(\"linear\")\n885 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00.000    1.0\n886 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00.500    0.0\n887 |         2023-03-01 07:00:01.000   -1.0\n888 |         2023-03-01 07:00:01.500    0.5\n889 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02.000    2.0\n890 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02.500    1.5\n891 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.000    1.0\n892 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.500    2.0\n893 |         2023-03-01 07:00:04.000    3.0\n894 |         Freq: 500ms, dtype: float64\n895 | \n896 |         Internal reindexing with ``asfreq()`` prior to interpolation leads to\n897 |         an interpolated timeseries on the basis of the reindexed timestamps\n898 |         (anchors). It is assured that all available datapoints from original\n899 |         series become anchors, so it also works for resampling-cases that lead\n900 |         to non-aligned timestamps, as in the following example:\n901 | \n902 |         >>> series.resample(\"400ms\").interpolate(\"linear\")\n903 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00.000    1.0\n904 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00.400    0.2\n905 |         2023-03-01 07:00:00.800   -0.6\n906 |         2023-03-01 07:00:01.200   -0.4\n907 |         2023-03-01 07:00:01.600    0.8\n908 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02.000    2.0\n909 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02.400    1.6\n910 |         2023-03-01 07:00:02.800    1.2\n911 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.200    1.4\n912 |         2023-03-01 07:00:03.600    2.2\n913 |         2023-03-01 07:00:04.000    3.0\n914 |         Freq: 400ms, dtype: float64\n915 | \n916 |         Note that the series correctly decreases between two anchors\n917 |         ``07:00:00`` and ``07:00:02``.\n918 |         \"\"\"\n919 |         assert downcast is lib.no_default  # just checking coverage\n920 |         result = self._upsample(\"asfreq\")\n921 | \n922 |         # If the original data has timestamps which are not aligned with the\n923 |         # target timestamps, we need to add those points back to the data frame\n924 |         # that is supposed to be interpolated. This does not work with\n925 |         # PeriodIndex, so we skip this case. GH#21351\n926 |         obj = self._selected_obj\n927 |         is_period_index = isinstance(obj.index, PeriodIndex)\n928 | \n929 |         # Skip this step for PeriodIndex\n930 |         if not is_period_index:\n931 |             final_index = result.index\n932 |             if isinstance(final_index, MultiIndex):\n933 |                 raise NotImplementedError(\n934 |                     \"Direct interpolation of MultiIndex data frames is not \"\n935 |                     \"supported. If you tried to resample and interpolate on a \"\n936 |                     \"grouped data frame, please use:\\n\"\n937 |                     \"`df.groupby(...).apply(lambda x: x.resample(...).\"\n938 |                     \"interpolate(...), include_groups=False)`\"\n939 |                     \"\\ninstead, as resampling and interpolation has to be \"\n940 |                     \"performed for each group independently.\"\n941 |                 )\n942 | \n943 |             missing_data_points_index = obj.index.difference(final_index)\n944 |             if len(missing_data_points_index) > 0:\n945 |                 result = concat(\n946 |                     [result, obj.loc[missing_data_points_index]]\n947 |                 ).sort_index()\n948 | \n949 |         result_interpolated = result.interpolate(\n950 |             method=method,\n951 |             axis=axis,\n952 |             limit=limit,\n953 |             inplace=inplace,\n954 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n955 |             limit_area=limit_area,\n956 |             downcast=downcast,\n957 |             **kwargs,\n958 |         )\n959 | \n960 |         # No further steps if the original data has a PeriodIndex\n961 |         if is_period_index:\n962 |             return result_interpolated\n963 | \n964 |         # Make sure that original data points which do not align with the\n965 |         # resampled index are removed\n966 |         result_interpolated = result_interpolated.loc[final_index]\n967 | \n968 |         # Make sure frequency indexes are preserved\n969 |         result_interpolated.index = final_index\n970 |         return result_interpolated\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.interpolate`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n7646 | def interpolate(\n7647 |         self,\n7648 |         method: InterpolateOptions = \"linear\",\n7649 |         *,\n7650 |         axis: Axis = 0,\n7651 |         limit: int | None = None,\n7652 |         inplace: bool = False,\n7653 |         limit_direction: Literal[\"forward\", \"backward\", \"both\"] | None = None,\n7654 |         limit_area: Literal[\"inside\", \"outside\"] | None = None,\n7655 |         **kwargs,\n7656 |     ) -> Self | None:\n7657 |         \"\"\"\n7658 |         Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n7659 | \n7660 |         Please note that only ``method='linear'`` is supported for\n7661 |         DataFrame/Series with a MultiIndex.\n7662 | \n7663 |         Parameters\n7664 |         ----------\n7665 |         method : str, default 'linear'\n7666 |             Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n7667 | \n7668 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally\n7669 |               spaced. This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n7670 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n7671 |               given length of interval.\n7672 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n7673 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic',\n7674 |               'barycentric', 'polynomial': Passed to\n7675 |               `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n7676 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n7677 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n7678 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n7679 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n7680 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n7681 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n7682 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n7683 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n7684 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n7685 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n7686 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n7687 | \n7688 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n7689 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n7690 |             and defaults to 0.\n7691 |         limit : int, optional\n7692 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n7693 |             0.\n7694 |         inplace : bool, default False\n7695 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n7696 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, optional, default 'forward'\n7697 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n7698 | \n7699 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n7700 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n7701 |             restriction.\n7702 | \n7703 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n7704 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n7705 |               (interpolate).\n7706 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n7707 | \n7708 |         **kwargs : optional\n7709 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n7710 | \n7711 |         Returns\n7712 |         -------\n7713 |         Series or DataFrame or None\n7714 |             Returns the same object type as the caller, interpolated at\n7715 |             some or all ``NaN`` values or None if ``inplace=True``.\n7716 | \n7717 |         See Also\n7718 |         --------\n7719 |         fillna : Fill missing values using different methods.\n7720 |         scipy.interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator : Piecewise cubic polynomials\n7721 |             (Akima interpolator).\n7722 |         scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives : Piecewise polynomial in the\n7723 |             Bernstein basis.\n7724 |         scipy.interpolate.interp1d : Interpolate a 1-D function.\n7725 |         scipy.interpolate.KroghInterpolator : Interpolate polynomial (Krogh\n7726 |             interpolator).\n7727 |         scipy.interpolate.PchipInterpolator : PCHIP 1-d monotonic cubic\n7728 |             interpolation.\n7729 |         scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline : Cubic spline data interpolator.\n7730 | \n7731 |         Notes\n7732 |         -----\n7733 |         The 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip' and 'akima'\n7734 |         methods are wrappers around the respective SciPy implementations of\n7735 |         similar names. These use the actual numerical values of the index.\n7736 |         For more information on their behavior, see the\n7737 |         `SciPy documentation\n7738 |         <https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/interpolate.html#univariate-interpolation>`__.\n7739 | \n7740 |         Examples\n7741 |         --------\n7742 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a :class:`~pandas.Series` via linear\n7743 |         interpolation.\n7744 | \n7745 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 1, np.nan, 3])\n7746 |         >>> s\n7747 |         0    0.0\n7748 |         1    1.0\n7749 |         2    NaN\n7750 |         3    3.0\n7751 |         dtype: float64\n7752 |         >>> s.interpolate()\n7753 |         0    0.0\n7754 |         1    1.0\n7755 |         2    2.0\n7756 |         3    3.0\n7757 |         dtype: float64\n7758 | \n7759 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a Series via polynomial interpolation or splines:\n7760 |         Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' methods require that you also specify\n7761 |         an ``order`` (int).\n7762 | \n7763 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 2, np.nan, 8])\n7764 |         >>> s.interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7765 |         0    0.000000\n7766 |         1    2.000000\n7767 |         2    4.666667\n7768 |         3    8.000000\n7769 |         dtype: float64\n7770 | \n7771 |         Fill the DataFrame forward (that is, going down) along each column\n7772 |         using linear interpolation.\n7773 | \n7774 |         Note how the last entry in column 'a' is interpolated differently,\n7775 |         because there is no entry after it to use for interpolation.\n7776 |         Note how the first entry in column 'b' remains ``NaN``, because there\n7777 |         is no entry before it to use for interpolation.\n7778 | \n7779 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n7780 |         ...     [\n7781 |         ...         (0.0, np.nan, -1.0, 1.0),\n7782 |         ...         (np.nan, 2.0, np.nan, np.nan),\n7783 |         ...         (2.0, 3.0, np.nan, 9.0),\n7784 |         ...         (np.nan, 4.0, -4.0, 16.0),\n7785 |         ...     ],\n7786 |         ...     columns=list(\"abcd\"),\n7787 |         ... )\n7788 |         >>> df\n7789 |              a    b    c     d\n7790 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7791 |         1  NaN  2.0  NaN   NaN\n7792 |         2  2.0  3.0  NaN   9.0\n7793 |         3  NaN  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7794 |         >>> df.interpolate(method=\"linear\", limit_direction=\"forward\", axis=0)\n7795 |              a    b    c     d\n7796 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7797 |         1  1.0  2.0 -2.0   5.0\n7798 |         2  2.0  3.0 -3.0   9.0\n7799 |         3  2.0  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7800 | \n7801 |         Using polynomial interpolation.\n7802 | \n7803 |         >>> df[\"d\"].interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7804 |         0     1.0\n7805 |         1     4.0\n7806 |         2     9.0\n7807 |         3    16.0\n7808 |         Name: d, dtype: float64\n7809 |         \"\"\"\n7810 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n7811 | \n7812 |         if inplace:\n7813 |             if not PYPY:\n7814 |                 if sys.getrefcount(self) <= REF_COUNT:\n7815 |                     warnings.warn(\n7816 |                         _chained_assignment_method_msg,\n7817 |                         ChainedAssignmentError,\n7818 |                         stacklevel=2,\n7819 |                     )\n7820 | \n7821 |         axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n7822 | \n7823 |         if self.empty:\n7824 |             if inplace:\n7825 |                 return None\n7826 |             return self.copy()\n7827 | \n7828 |         if not isinstance(method, str):\n7829 |             raise ValueError(\"'method' should be a string, not None.\")\n7830 | \n7831 |         obj, should_transpose = (self.T, True) if axis == 1 else (self, False)\n7832 | \n7833 |         if isinstance(obj.index, MultiIndex) and method != \"linear\":\n7834 |             raise ValueError(\n7835 |                 \"Only `method=linear` interpolation is supported on MultiIndexes.\"\n7836 |             )\n7837 | \n7838 |         limit_direction = missing.infer_limit_direction(limit_direction, method)\n7839 | \n7840 |         index = missing.get_interp_index(method, obj.index)\n7841 |         new_data = obj._mgr.interpolate(\n7842 |             method=method,\n7843 |             index=index,\n7844 |             limit=limit,\n7845 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n7846 |             limit_area=limit_area,\n7847 |             inplace=inplace,\n7848 |             **kwargs,\n7849 |         )\n7850 | \n7851 |         result = self._constructor_from_mgr(new_data, axes=new_data.axes)\n7852 |         if should_transpose:\n7853 |             result = result.T\n7854 |         if inplace:\n7855 |             return self._update_inplace(result)\n7856 |         else:\n7857 |             return result.__finalize__(self, method=\"interpolate\")\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/arrays/base.py:ExtensionArray.interpolate`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 989 | def interpolate(\n 990 |         self,\n 991 |         *,\n 992 |         method: InterpolateOptions,\n 993 |         axis: int,\n 994 |         index: Index,\n 995 |         limit,\n 996 |         limit_direction,\n 997 |         limit_area,\n 998 |         copy: bool,\n 999 |         **kwargs,\n1000 |     ) -> Self:\n1001 |         \"\"\"\n1002 |         Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n1003 | \n1004 |         Parameters\n1005 |         ----------\n1006 |         method : str, default 'linear'\n1007 |             Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n1008 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally spaced.\n1009 |             This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n1010 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n1011 |             given length of interval.\n1012 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n1013 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic', 'barycentric',\n1014 |             'polynomial': Passed to scipy.interpolate.interp1d, whereas 'spline'\n1015 |             is passed to scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline. These methods use\n1016 |             the numerical values of the index.\n1017 |             Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that you also specify an\n1018 |             order (int), e.g. arr.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5).\n1019 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n1020 |             'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods\n1021 |             of similar names. See Notes.\n1022 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives.\n1023 |         axis : int\n1024 |             Axis to interpolate along. For 1-dimensional data, use 0.\n1025 |         index : Index\n1026 |             Index to use for